cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Grounded verification of materials-chemistry reasoning: detection is the bottleneck, repair is easy"
abstract: >-
  Large language models confabulate chemical and materials objects (molecular
  formulas, space groups, formation energies) inside fluent reasoning traces,
  most often for the long-tail entities where verification matters most. We build
  a tiered, deterministic verifier that grounds each extractable claim against
  authoritative databases and physics, and study it across four current models on
  528 condition-pinned prompts. Gated correction removes confabulated formulas
  (error-given-commitment 22% to 4%, p=7×10^-22) at 3.2× fewer retrievals than
  blanket augmentation and beats a conversational oracle retriever end-to-end; an
  extraction-enforcing oracle variant that forces value restatement is
  complementary, solving copyable quantitative claims (formation-energy error to
  1%) where gated correction is detection-limited. Repair is easy everywhere
  (80–97%); in-loop detection recall is the bottleneck, since a consistency stage
  raises it from 19% to 94%, though without an injected reference no accuracy lift
  follows. Grounding lifts downstream accuracy only when the verifier's scope
  reaches the deliverable (83% to 90%). The lift tracks baseline error, governed
  by detection recall not model openness, and transfers with orchestration-layer
  changes only: absent on near-ceiling constants, large on isotope half-lives
  (11% to 0%), where two frontier-closed models show a first significant lift.
